1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,123
Pick the feature you're most confident about, not your simplest one.

2
00:00:04,123 --> 00:00:04,844
Why that one?

3
00:00:04,844 --> 00:00:08,506
Confidence untested is exactly what this workshop exists to check.

4
00:00:08,506 --> 00:00:13,000
Open A11, and give this the same twenty minutes every workshop in this course has

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:13,555
asked for.

6
00:00:13,555 --> 00:00:14,942
The bar is identical too.

7
00:00:14,942 --> 00:00:15,608
Not perfect.

8
00:00:15,608 --> 00:00:16,107
Complete.

9
00:00:16,107 --> 00:00:20,213
Five specific findings, one per category, even where the honest finding is

10
00:00:20,213 --> 00:00:24,486
"measured, and it's fine," which is a completely different, far more valuable

11
00:00:24,486 --> 00:00:28,924
sentence than "probably fine, never checked." Red-teaming your own feature feels

12
00:00:28,924 --> 00:00:30,422
adversarial, and it should.

13
00:00:30,422 --> 00:00:34,972
You are looking for the thing that's wrong, not confirming the thing that's right.

14
00:00:34,972 --> 00:00:39,577
That's a different mindset from most product work, and it's worth naming before you

15
00:00:39,577 --> 00:00:43,073
start, so the exercise doesn't quietly turn into a victory lap.

16
00:00:43,073 --> 00:00:47,400
This is also the only lecture in this section that asks you to attack your own

17
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,065
feature rather than assess it.

18
00:00:49,065 --> 00:00:51,229
Assessment can be generous by accident.

19
00:00:51,229 --> 00:00:55,889
An actual attempted injection either works or it doesn't, and there's no comfortable

20
00:00:55,889 --> 00:00:57,554
middle ground to retreat into.

21
00:00:58,104 --> 00:01:02,447
Notice the first block asks you to actually try an injection, not discuss whether

22
00:01:02,447 --> 00:01:05,129
your feature is vulnerable to one in the abstract.

23
00:01:05,129 --> 00:01:09,579
Write one sentence that looks like ordinary content and see whether it changes what

24
00:01:09,579 --> 00:01:10,545
your feature does.

25
00:01:10,545 --> 00:01:13,226
That's the entire test, and it takes four minutes.

26
00:01:13,226 --> 00:01:17,516
The second block moves through three separate questions fast, and that's fine as

27
00:01:17,516 --> 00:01:21,966
long as all three genuinely get answered rather than folded into one blended "we're

28
00:01:21,966 --> 00:01:23,468
careful with data" sentence.

29
00:01:23,468 --> 00:01:27,919
Trace one real request for the first, open the actual vendor setting for the third,

30
00:01:27,919 --> 00:01:31,511
and resist answering all three with the same comfortable paragraph.

31
00:01:31,511 --> 00:01:35,962
The middle block, the EU AI Act tier, moves fast for most features and that's fine.

32
00:01:35,962 --> 00:01:40,466
Landing in minimal or limited risk in under four minutes is a genuine finding, not a

33
00:01:40,466 --> 00:01:41,807
block you rushed through.

34
00:01:41,807 --> 00:01:46,311
The feature that takes the full four minutes to sort is the one worth a second look.

35
00:01:46,861 --> 00:01:51,548
Minutes twelve through sixteen ask a question you can only answer by opening your

36
00:01:51,548 --> 00:01:54,152
actual golden set, not by reasoning about it.

37
00:01:54,152 --> 00:01:56,408
Count the cases in your chosen segment.

38
00:01:56,408 --> 00:02:01,095
If the honest count is close to zero, that's the finding, immediately, no further

39
00:02:01,095 --> 00:02:02,021
analysis needed.

40
00:02:02,021 --> 00:02:06,476
The last block closes the loop back to 8.1's very first row: nobody owns what

41
00:02:06,476 --> 00:02:07,807
happens when it breaks.

42
00:02:07,807 --> 00:02:12,668
By minute twenty, that row should have an actual name next to it, or an honest blank

43
00:02:12,668 --> 00:02:15,271
you're committing to fill by a specific date.

44
00:02:15,821 --> 00:02:17,017
Recognise this shape.

45
00:02:17,017 --> 00:02:21,572
It's 8.1's own example of what a closeable finding sounds like, and it's roughly

46
00:02:21,572 --> 00:02:25,671
what each of your five findings today should read like by minute twenty.

47
00:02:25,671 --> 00:02:30,454
Notice what the sentence contains: the exact input path, named, specific enough that

48
00:02:30,454 --> 00:02:35,123
an engineer reading it knows exactly what to fix without asking a single follow-up

49
00:02:35,123 --> 00:02:35,635
question.

50
00:02:35,635 --> 00:02:40,361
Notice what it isn't: "prompt injection is a risk we should think about," the vague

51
00:02:40,361 --> 00:02:43,948
version this exact sentence was written to replace back in 8.1.

52
00:02:43,948 --> 00:02:47,194
Hold every one of today's five findings to this standard.

53
00:02:47,194 --> 00:02:49,642
"PII handling seems fine" is not a finding.

54
00:02:49,642 --> 00:02:54,026
"Support ticket logs retain the caller's phone number for ninety days with no

55
00:02:54,026 --> 00:02:54,823
redaction" is.

56
00:02:54,823 --> 00:02:58,069
Only the second one tells anyone what to actually go fix.

57
00:02:58,069 --> 00:03:02,567
Compare it, too, against the register row it replaced, the one 8.1 opened with:

58
00:03:02,567 --> 00:03:06,951
"prompt injection is a risk we should think about," owned by no one, reviewed

59
00:03:06,951 --> 00:03:08,545
whenever someone remembered.

60
00:03:08,545 --> 00:03:12,701
Twenty minutes and one honest attempt turned that sentence into this one.

61
00:03:12,701 --> 00:03:17,142
That's the entire distance this workshop is asking you to cover, five separate

62
00:03:17,142 --> 00:03:17,484
times.

63
00:03:18,034 --> 00:03:22,436
Say the honest caveat, because the instinct under time pressure is to write the

64
00:03:22,436 --> 00:03:26,726
reassuring answer rather than the true one, and this workshop is specifically

65
00:03:26,726 --> 00:03:29,010
testing your resistance to that instinct.

66
00:03:29,010 --> 00:03:33,634
A clean pass in all five categories is a wonderful outcome, if it's actually earned

67
00:03:33,634 --> 00:03:34,303
by checking.

68
00:03:34,303 --> 00:03:38,927
The same five clean boxes, filled in from confidence rather than a check, are worse

69
00:03:38,927 --> 00:03:43,273
than useless, because they'll be trusted exactly as much as if they were real.

70
00:03:43,273 --> 00:03:47,508
If minute twenty arrives and a block is genuinely unfinished, write "not yet

71
00:03:47,508 --> 00:03:48,733
measured" and move on.

72
00:03:48,733 --> 00:03:53,358
That sentence, honestly written, is worth more to whoever reads this afterward than

73
00:03:53,358 --> 00:03:55,085
a guess dressed up as a result.

74
00:03:55,085 --> 00:03:59,598
This is worth saying twice in one section, because it's the exact same caveat 8.1

75
00:03:59,598 --> 00:04:03,832
opened with, aimed at yourself this time instead of a hypothetical teammate.

76
00:04:03,832 --> 00:04:08,345
Under time pressure, in your own workshop, the temptation to write the reassuring

77
00:04:08,345 --> 00:04:11,633
row is just as real as it was reading about someone else's.

78
00:04:12,183 --> 00:04:16,574
That's today's bar, held to the same standard as every workshop before this one:

79
00:04:16,574 --> 00:04:19,154
attempted honestly, not performed convincingly.

80
00:04:19,154 --> 00:04:21,899
If you finish with time left, don't polish evenly.

81
00:04:21,899 --> 00:04:26,456
Return to whichever block you moved through fastest, because speed in this specific

82
00:04:26,456 --> 00:04:30,957
exercise is usually a sign of an assumption sliding through unchecked, not genuine

83
00:04:30,957 --> 00:04:32,549
confidence earned by a check.

84
00:04:32,549 --> 00:04:37,105
Resist the urge to write a summary paragraph at the end declaring the feature safe.

85
00:04:37,105 --> 00:04:41,497
That sentence belongs to nobody, and it undoes the entire point of five specific

86
00:04:41,497 --> 00:04:44,022
findings by replacing them with one vague one.

87
00:04:44,022 --> 00:04:48,579
Most people running this exercise for the first time find at least one block harder

88
00:04:48,579 --> 00:04:52,586
than expected, usually the one they were most confident about walking in.

89
00:04:52,586 --> 00:04:53,903
That's not a bad result.

90
00:04:53,903 --> 00:04:58,021
It's the entire reason a red-team pass is worth twenty minutes instead of a

91
00:04:58,021 --> 00:04:59,942
five-second gut check in a hallway.

92
00:05:00,492 --> 00:05:02,085
One thing before you move on.

93
00:05:02,085 --> 00:05:06,481
Finish all five blocks for real, including the parts that feel unnecessary for a

94
00:05:06,481 --> 00:05:08,019
feature you're confident in.

95
00:05:08,019 --> 00:05:12,195
Confidence is exactly what a red-team exercise is built to test, not assume.

96
00:05:12,195 --> 00:05:16,645
Then take every gap you found, however small, and give it a name, an owner, and a

97
00:05:16,645 --> 00:05:17,909
date in 8.1's register.

98
00:05:17,909 --> 00:05:21,810
A red-team pass that produces findings nobody follows up on is theatre.

99
00:05:21,810 --> 00:05:25,491
One that produces five dated rows is this section actually working.

100
00:05:25,491 --> 00:05:29,997
None of this needs to happen today for every feature you own, only for the one you

101
00:05:29,997 --> 00:05:32,249
actually opened A11 against this session.

102
00:05:32,249 --> 00:05:36,590
Repeat the pass on your next feature before it ships, not after, and the twenty

103
00:05:36,590 --> 00:05:38,898
minutes gets faster every time you run it.

104
00:05:38,898 --> 00:05:43,348
Next lecture closes this section: what these six lectures actually built, and how

105
00:05:43,348 --> 00:05:47,634
directly it plugs into analytics next, where you'll learn to measure whether a

106
00:05:47,634 --> 00:05:50,875
feature is actually succeeding, not just whether it's safe.
